{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How many times can I half the distance between my hands? Assuming perfect coordination and the ability  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to localize my palms to the quantum level. 15 halvings gets them to within a cell’s width.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "33 to within a single atom, 50 and they’re a proton’s width apart. Half the distance 115  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19280,
    "dDurationMs": 8064,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times and they’re a single Planck-length apart - 1.6x10^-35 meters. Surely we can keep going - .8,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27344,
    "dDurationMs": 9296,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": ".4, .2 x10^-35 m? Bizarrely, those distances might not even exist in any meaningful way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40760,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": " "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41760,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no limit to the number \nof times you can half a number,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45120,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the same might not be true of space. The planck length is thought to represent the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minimum length for which the concept \nof length is even meaningful. Here,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55360,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the illusion that space is smooth and continuous breaks down. But what happens when you go smaller?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62480,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does space break into discrete chunks? Does space even exist as we know it? Before we zoom in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69760,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on space, let’s zoom back in time to see where the idea of the Planck length even came from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76080,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the final year of the 19th century, \nMax Planck ushered in the quantum age by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thinking about hot pokers. He found the \nlong-sought mathematical description of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84320,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blackbody, or thermal radiation - by requiring that the energy of light in this heat-glow was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not infinitely divisible. Rather came in quanta - chunks of energy that we now call photons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98160,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck’s discovery hinges on a single \nnumber that appears in his equation - the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck constant. It represents the \nchunkiness of thermal radiation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Multiply the frequency of the light by this \nnumber and you get the energy of a single photon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112080,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Max Planck had introduced the whole quantized light thing as a mathematical trick. He expected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the value of the Planck constant to be zero, which would mean that energy could be infinitely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "divided. But the constant remained stubbornly non-zero, even if it’s very, very small. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129520,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simple fact revealed the fundamental blockiness of the subatomic world. We now see it everywhere  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in quantum mechanics - it’s a fundamental constant of nature that defines the scale of the quantum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141840,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From the Planck constant comes the Planck length.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143840,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s the length you get when you combine the gravitational constant, the speed of light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147280,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Planck’s constant in just the right way \nto give units of length - the square root  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151200,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of G times h-bar over c^3. Where h-bar is \njust the Planck constant divided by 2 pi."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158560,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you now know, the number comes out pretty small - around 10^-35 m. But why is this random-seeming  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "combination of constants so important? Well, it represents the scale at which space itself  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171920,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is thought to “become quantum”. I say \n“thought” because we’ve never been able  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to conduct experiments at that tiny scale. So to understand why physicists believe this length is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so important, we’ll have to do some experiments in our brains. Follow me through a couple of thought  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiments - or gedankenexperiments as a good German physicist like Max Planck would call them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194400,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start, let’s say you’re trying to measure the distance to an object. You shine a laser beam at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199520,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it, which reflects back to a detector that records the light travel time, which also gives you the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204640,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distance because you know the speed of light. But that distance measure has an uncertainty because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can only clock the instant of the return to within one wave-cycle of the electromagnetic wave.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives a distance uncertainty of around one wavelength of whatever type of light you’re  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using. OK, easy enough - just use a very short wavelength to get a very precise distance measure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now you have a new problem. Light carries energy and momentum - and the shorter the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231760,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelength, the more it carries. If you bombard your object with a powerful short-wavelength X-ray  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237200,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "laser and you may get an amazing distance measure, but you’ll transfer a lot of momentum. You can try  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244160,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turning down the power of your beam, but remember that the minimum intensity is when you have only a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248800,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "single photon. And because you can’t know exactly how the momentum transfer will happen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll always have an uncertainty in the \nparticle’s final momentum that’s roughly equal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the momentum of the single photon. You may see where this is going - we’re about to discover  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Heisenberg uncertainty principle. Bare \nwith me though with just a touch of mathematics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270000,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A photon’s momentum is the Planck \nconstant divided by its wavelength.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273760,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So just replace photon momentum with the uncertainty momentum of the measured object,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and replace wavelength with uncertainty \nin its position. Rearrange and voila,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283680,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg. Or close enough - A proper \nderivation gets you a factor of 1 over 4 pi."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was the line of reasoning that Heisenberg himself went through, and we call this thought  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experiment the Heisenberg microscope. We now know that the uncertainty principle is far more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298480,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental than just the effect of disturbing the \nsystem that you’re trying to measure - and we’ve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explored that previously. It also applies to other pairs of variables besides position and momentum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308560,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The point I want to focus on today is that through the uncertainty principle we see that the Planck  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312960,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant represents the limit to which we can measure the universe. We recently talked about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318560,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how with a bit of clever physicsing it’s possible to stretch the uncertainty principle to the limit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, we should be able to measure location in space down to any conceivable precision,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329280,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps even infinite precision - as long as \nwe’re happy to have infinite uncertainty in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333600,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum. But it turns out that the Planck constant defines a new source of quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338000,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty that you can’t ever physics away. And we hit that uncertainty at the Planck length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s continue our gerdankenexperiment, \nnow adding two key ideas from Einstein:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first that mass and energy are equivalent, as expressed by the most famous equation ever, E=mc2;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and second, that mass and energy warp the fabric of spacetime. So back to Heisenberg’s microscope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362160,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s say we’re trying to measure our distance \nwith perfect precision, and who cares about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366000,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "momentum. We keep decreasing the wavelength of our measuring photon - ultraviolet - X-ray -  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371800,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gamma-ray - which also increases the photon’s energy and momentum. As we crank up the energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377520,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even further we start to notice something. The photon is starting to produce an observable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382480,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field. Even though photons are massless, if enclosed in a system a photon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "creates what we call effective mass, according to Einstein’s famous equation. The resulting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393840,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational field changes the distance to the object, adding a new uncertainty to the distance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399200,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay, one last dip into the math to see how big this uncertainty is. Space is stretched  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403840,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by a factor equal to the effective mass times the gravitational constant divided by c^2.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409440,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s replace the mass with the effective \nmass of our photon - its energy over c^2,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the energy of a photon is Planck’s \nconstant times c^2 over the wavelength.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have this thing that’s full of our \nwonderful fundamental constants - in fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the exact form as the Planck length \nsquared divided by the wavelength."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430080,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where, I hope, it gets interesting. As you pump up the energy of your photon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reducing its wavelength also reduces the regular Heisenberg uncertainty, but at the same time this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new uncertainty increases. You’re still winning the uncertainty game - up to a point. When the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448640,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wavelength of the photon reaches exactly the Planck length these two uncertainty terms become  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same, and any further decrease in photon wavelength actually increases your measurement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459040,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uncertainty due to the warping of space. So this is one way of thinking about it - the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463280,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck length represents the best possible \nresolution that any distance can be measured."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also represents the minimum size that \nyou can meaningfully ascribe to anything.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473840,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine now that you’re trying to measure \nthe distance across a one-Planck-length  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478080,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object. You need a photon with a \nwavelength smaller than one-Planck-length.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that photon has enough effective mass to produce a black hole with a Planck-length event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon - so any attempt to measure something that small swallows it in a black hole.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another way to think about that is that if you try to measure a size smaller than the Planck length,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498720,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the warped geometry changes that size to give 100% uncertainty. This explains why the Planck length  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is this combination of fundamental constants - it’s the wavelength of a photon that creates a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole of the same size, and so represents the fundamental limit of the measurability of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517680,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To convince you that this really is fundamental,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let me give you one more example that doesn’t involve light. Imagine we want to precisely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526240,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "define the position of an elementary particle - let’s say an electron. To define its position  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531600,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to be able to say that all of the \nelectron’s mass is within a certain volume.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536480,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So defining position means shrinking \nthat volume down as much as possible.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the Heisenberg uncertainty principle says \nthat when we do that we increase the uncertainty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in momentum of that bundle of electron-ness. That also means increasing the uncertainty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 551840,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in energy density in that volume. By the time we localize the electron to within one Planck length,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557920,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the uncertainty in energy is about equal to the entire mass-energy of the electron. At that point,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can’t say there’s just one electron. The \nuncertainty in the energy induces a process called  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair production. The act of localiuzing the \nelectron causes pairs of virtual electrons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576160,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and positrons to appear around it. The positron will annihilate with either the new electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or the original. This happens continuously, so it looks like the electron flits around in position,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587280,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thwarting any attempt to fix its location. \nFor any particle, this uncertainty due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592800,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this pair production occurs when you localize its energy to within a Planck-length-diameter volume."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the universe seems to be conspiring to stop us measuring distances or sizes smaller than the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Planck length. But now we get to the big question: does that mean that smaller sizes/lengths/chunks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space don’t exist? Does that mean space is not continuous on the tiniest scales?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617680,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That might be true, but everything I’ve said \ntoday doesn’t quite get us that far. What we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say is that distances are undefined \non the Planck scale. At that scale,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628640,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Heisenberg’s principle tells us that the \ncurvature of space is fundamentally uncertain.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the same way that you get virtual particles on subatomic scales, on the Planck scale you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638480,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get virtual spacetime fluctuations, and \neven virtual black holes and wormholes - a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643840,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluctuating roil of spacetime that John Archibald Wheeler called the spacetime foam. At least,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650000,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s the case if you try to define a continuous fabric of space beyond that Planck Length."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655840,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we don’t really know how well this picture represents space on this scale. The nature of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661120,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space is defined by Einstein’s general relativity, and GR breaks down at the Planck scale because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667760,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can’t sensibly define distances. We think that space AND time - spacetime - “go quantum” at that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scale - but we just don’t know in what way. We need a theory of quantum gravity to answer this.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll come back to what the contending theories say about the true nature of space another time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686560,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is there a smallest length? Well there’s a smallest meaningful length, at least for any  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intuitive conception of space. Quantum uncertainty thwarts our attempt to understand the universe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by simply splitting it into smaller parts. You’ll have to stay tuned to the show, and to the future  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704880,
    "dDurationMs": 7596,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics, to find out what might lie beneath the smallest possible scale of measurable spacetime."
    } ]
  } ]
}
